WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:06.919
Speaker 1: This is interesting because it's
one of those stories where there is not one

00:00:06.919 --> 00:00:11.030
simple understanding of the world that tells
us what we need to know.

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:16.240
Sometimes we need to hold sort of two ideas
that might appear to conflict simultaneously.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:22.829
And we have a sort of bifurcation, maybe I
would call it, in the United States right

00:00:22.829 --> 00:00:28.869
now in terms of the Republican Party, where
on the one hand, Donald Trump is still clearly

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:35.020
the leader of the Republican Party until proven
otherwise, and yet he is rapidly sliding towards

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:36.550
online irrelevance.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:38.730
Now, you might hear that and say, David.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:43.870
Sir, how could that be true, how could Trump
be irrelevant online, but be the revered holy

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:46.320
leader of the Republican Party?

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.990
And the answer is something that we learned
through the 20 20 Democratic primary, which

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:55.290
is that the real world and the online world
are two different things, which remember when

00:00:55.290 --> 00:01:03.730
Bernie Sanders appeared to be dominating Twitter,
Twitter or Reddit during the 20 20 primary,

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:07.990
and yet Joe Biden won the Democratic primary
in 2020 by quite a bit.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:13.930
We realized what happens online is not necessarily
reflective of what's going on in the greater

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:15.280
real world.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:18.290
So on the one hand, most Republicans still
like Trump.

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.530
Most Republicans want Trump involved in politics.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:23.380
Most Republicans want Trump to run in 2024.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:25.210
We see this in every poll.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:29.060
Most Republicans still believe Trump did win
20 20.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:33.140
Most Republicans say they would follow Trump
to his own third party if Trump were to leave

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:34.909
the Republican Party.

00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:38.160
Most Republicans say if given the opportunity
in twenty twenty four, they would vote for

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:39.960
Donald Trump again.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:44.720
But then look at what's happening online,
thanks to being banned from Twitter, Facebook,

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:50.490
YouTube and many other platforms for his regular
rule breaking and incitement to violence,

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:56.520
Donald Trump has been relegated to his blog
at Donald Trump Dotcom.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:00.680
And that blog is having essentially zero impact.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.320
We barely talk about it.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.700
We mentioned today Trump's reaction to the
grand jury being convened against them.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:07.700
That's big news.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:13.010
A grand jury looking at whether a former president
committed crimes, but it's barely making news,

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:15.440
what Donald Trump writes on his blog.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:22.870
Remember that Trump and his staff were regularly
teasing how Trump's new online social media

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:29.500
communication platform is going to revolutionize
everything and put Trump back in the spotlight.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.340
It ended up being just a blog that barely
anybody is reading.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:39.030
And as The Washington Post now reports, online
discussions of Trump have plunged to a five

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.650
year low in the last week.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:50.310
Trump's website attracted fewer estimated
visitors than the pet adoption website Petfinder

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.530
and the recipe website Delish.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:58.760
So the incredible sort of fork at which we're
arriving is that while Trump is clearly the

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:04.020
Republican Party's biggest star at this point
in time and many Republican lawmakers continue

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:10.240
to pledge endless loyalty to him, it is simply
not translating to online influence at all.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:15.440
Overall, online influence for Trump is down
about 95 percent since January and down to

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.800
its lowest level since 2016.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:23.989
His blog from the desk of Donald Trump, which
they have been so heavily promoting.

00:03:23.989 --> 00:03:25.870
Sometimes the blogs are only shared on Facebook.

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:32.099
Two thousand times we get sometimes 2000 shares
or more of a random video that we publish

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:33.099
to Facebook.

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:35.220
So understand the scale of these numbers.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:38.120
These are really low numbers for a former
president.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:44.640
So, of course, the real question is, absent
his ability to make stories viral, is Trump

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:49.420
going to lose control of the narrative as
we get towards twenty twenty two?

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.870
How does this affect the Republican Party
in twenty twenty two?

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:55.599
How does it affect the Republican Party and
Trump in twenty twenty four?

00:03:55.599 --> 00:03:59.680
Can Trump wield the influence he wants to
wield and be the kingmaker he wants to be

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:03.860
in twenty twenty two without that bully pulpit,
I don't know that he can.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:08.650
Now in some sense, Trump may realize already
that he needs to do something because we have

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:16.160
reports that he's planning to, I guess, get
his old the dark blue 757, the Boeing 757

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:17.160
airplane.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:22.290
He's planning to get it back into action for
so-called rallies and for who the rallies

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.300
will be and when.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:25.300
We don't really know.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:32.960
I do think that if we close in on twenty twenty
two and Trump starts doing his fly in rallies

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:39.300
around the country in support of Republican
candidates that he's deemed to be acceptable

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:44.220
Republicans, that will get him attention because
a former president on the campaign trail gets

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:45.220
attention.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:46.410
That's just the reality.

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:52.500
But if you doubt at all the reach of Trump's
influence today, look at what happened with

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:53.890
Liz Cheney.

00:04:53.890 --> 00:05:00.020
Liz Cheney was removed from House Republican
leadership by Republicans because she publicly

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:01.979
admitted, hey, guys, Trump just lost.

00:05:01.979 --> 00:05:03.270
He just lost.

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:06.070
She was with Trump 93 percent of the time
on voting.

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:11.440
It's not like she's some liberal, but she
acknowledged Trump lost and she was kicked

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:13.150
out of Republican leadership.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:19.190
That simply doesn't happen if Donald Trump
has lost control of his party.

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:24.400
And that is continued proof that Donald Trump
for now has not lost control of his party.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.470
He's losing the bully pulpit.

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:30.900
He's lost the majority of his online influence,
but he has not lost control of the Republican

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:54.889
Party, at least 

00:05:54.889 --> 00:06:27.039
for now.

